{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>לְמָשְׁחָה בָהֶם</b> (to anoint with them) — to exalt oneself with them. For there is an 'anointing' that is an expression of dominion, as in 'לְךָ נְתַתִּים לְמָשְׁחָה' (Numbers 18:8), 'אַל תִּגְּעוּ בִמְשִׁיחָי' (Psalms 105:15). This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 29:29). And perhaps this is indeed so, for since the governance of Israel belongs to the anointed — to the king and to the High Priest — they extend the term to all authority. And likewise, 'וּמָשַׁחְתָּ אֶת חֲזָאֵל לְמֶלֶךְ עַל אֲרָם, וְאֶת אֱלִישָׁע בֶּן שָׁפָט תִּמְשַׁח לְנָבִיא תַּחְתֶּיךָ' (I Kings 19:15–16). But here the correct meaning is 'לְמָשְׁחָה בָהֶם' — to anoint with [these garments] the priests, the sons who become High Priests, 'וּלְמַלֵּא בָם אֶת יָדָם' — to offer the sacrifices through them. And likewise, 'לְךָ נְתַתִּים לְמָשְׁחָה' — I have given them to be yours because I anointed you as a priest to Me. And so too 'אַל תִּגְּעוּ בִמְשִׁיחָי' — for whoever touches them touches the anointed ones of HaShem who are destined to come forth from them, as He said, 'וּמְלָכִים מֵחֲלָצֶיךָ יֵצֵאוּ' (Genesis 35:11), 'מַלְכֵי עַמִּים מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ' (ibid. 17:16). And it is possible that 'וּמָשַׁחְתָּ אֶת חֲזָאֵל לְמֶלֶךְ עַל אֲרָם' is similarly said because [Elijah] would send him oil to anoint him as king, to inform him that it is the word of HaShem; and Elisha did so at his master's command, as he told him that he would reign (II Kings 8:13), even though it was not written there explicitly, and he anointed Elisha as prophet. And perhaps they did likewise with Cyrus, whom they anointed like the kings of Israel, so that he would know that a prophet in Israel had appointed him and had proclaimed his name for the sake of HaShem. Therefore it says, 'לִמְשִׁיחוֹ לְכֹרֶשׁ' (Isaiah 45:1). And our Sages said (Megillah 12a), 'And was Cyrus then a מָשִׁיחַ?' etc. And behold, all of these were actual anointings. But the verse that says (Isaiah 61:1), 'רוּחַ ה׳ אֱלֹהִים עָלַי יַעַן מָשַׁח ה׳ אֹתִי לְבַשֵּׂר עֲנָוִים' — this is by way of allegory, likening the spirit of holiness that rests upon him to good oil, as in 'טוֹב שֵׁם מִשֶּׁמֶן טוֹב' (Ecclesiastes 7:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:18:37.682537483Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}